+
+Semantic Clustering
+-------------------
+
+Embeddings that are close in vector space carry similar meanings, which makes them a natural fit for
+**clustering**: grouping a corpus into coherent topics without any labels. The
+:mod:`sentence_transformers.util.clustering` module provides deterministic implementations of the two
+most common clustering strategies, plus helpers for inspecting the results:
+
+- :func:`~sentence_transformers.util.kmeans_clustering` partitions the corpus into a fixed number of
+  clusters. It uses a seeded k-means++ initialization, so repeated runs on the same data produce
+  identical clusters.
+- :func:`~sentence_transformers.util.agglomerative_clustering` builds the clusters bottom-up and
+  stops either at a target number of clusters or when the closest clusters are farther apart than a
+  distance threshold. The ``linkage`` parameter controls how distances between clusters are computed
+  (``"single"``, ``"complete"``, or ``"average"``).
+
+::
+
+    from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer
+    from sentence_transformers.util import agglomerative_clustering, kmeans_clustering
+
+    model = SentenceTransformer("all-MiniLM-L6-v2")
+    embeddings = model.encode(corpus)
+
+    # 1) k-means with a fixed number of clusters
+    clustering = kmeans_clustering(embeddings, num_clusters=10, seed=42)
+    print(clustering.num_clusters, clustering.cluster_sizes)
+    for cluster_id, indices in clustering.largest_clusters(3):
+        print(f"Cluster {cluster_id}: {[corpus[i] for i in indices[:5]]}")
+
+    # 2) Agglomerative clustering with a distance threshold: the number of clusters
+    #    emerges from the data instead of being fixed upfront.
+    clustering = agglomerative_clustering(embeddings, threshold=0.3, linkage="average")
+    print(f"Found {clustering.num_clusters} clusters")
+
+Both functions return a :class:`~sentence_transformers.util.clustering.ClusteringResult`, which maps
+every input index to a cluster id (``result.labels``) and offers convenience views such as
+``result.clusters``, ``result.cluster_sizes`` and ``result.largest_clusters(n)``. Cluster ids are
+normalized so that the cluster containing the first input is always cluster 0.
+
+To extract the most typical sentences of every cluster, use
+:func:`~sentence_transformers.util.select_representatives`, which picks the members closest to each
+cluster centroid::
+
+    from sentence_transformers.util import select_representatives
+
+    representatives = select_representatives(embeddings, clustering, top_k=3)
+    for cluster_id, indices in representatives.items():
+        print(f"Cluster {cluster_id} is about: {[corpus[i] for i in indices]}")
+
+Semantic Deduplication
+----------------------
+
+Real-world corpora often contain (near-)duplicates: boilerplate, reposts, slightly edited copies.
+:func:`~sentence_transformers.util.semantic_deduplication` flags embeddings whose cosine similarity
+exceeds a threshold and resolves them into duplicate groups with a union-find pass, so duplicates are
+detected **transitively**: if A matches B and B matches C, all three land in one group even if A and
+C never pass the threshold directly. From every group the earliest sentence is kept as the
+representative::
+
+    from sentence_transformers.util import semantic_deduplication
+
+    result = semantic_deduplication(embeddings, threshold=0.9)
+    unique_corpus = result.apply(corpus)
+    print(f"Kept {result.num_kept} of {len(corpus)} sentences")
+    for removed, kept in result.duplicate_of.items():
+        print(f"{corpus[removed]!r} is a duplicate of {corpus[kept]!r}")
+
+The returned :class:`~sentence_transformers.util.clustering.DeduplicationResult` exposes
+``keep_indices`` / ``remove_indices`` for index-based filtering, ``duplicate_of`` for the
+removed-to-representative mapping, and ``groups`` for the full duplicate groups.
+
+Evaluating Clustering Quality
+-----------------------------
+
+When gold labels are available (e.g. topic or intent annotations), the clustering can be scored
+against them. The metric helpers in :mod:`sentence_transformers.util.clustering` cover the standard
+clustering metrics:
+
+- :func:`~sentence_transformers.util.v_measure_score`: harmonic mean of homogeneity (clusters contain
+  a single class) and completeness (a class lives in a single cluster);
+- :func:`~sentence_transformers.util.homogeneity_score` and
+  :func:`~sentence_transformers.util.completeness_score`: the two components individually;
+- :func:`~sentence_transformers.util.adjusted_rand_score`: chance-corrected agreement between the two
+  partitions;
+- :func:`~sentence_transformers.util.normalized_mutual_info_score`: shared information between gold
+  labels and clusters;
+- :func:`~sentence_transformers.util.fowlkes_mallows_score`: geometric mean of pairwise precision and
+  recall;
+- :func:`~sentence_transformers.util.purity_score`: majority-class purity per cluster;
+- :func:`~sentence_transformers.util.cluster_accuracy_score`: classification accuracy after matching
+  clusters to labels with an optimal (Hungarian) assignment.
+
+::
+
+    from sentence_transformers.util import v_measure_score
+
+    clustering = kmeans_clustering(embeddings, num_clusters=len(set(gold_labels)))
+    score = v_measure_score(gold_labels, clustering.labels)
+    print(f"V-measure: {score:.4f}")
+
+For monitoring a model *during training*,
+:class:`~sentence_transformers.sentence_transformer.evaluation.ClusteringEvaluator` wraps the whole
+pipeline: it encodes a held-out set of sentences, clusters the embeddings, scores the clusters
+against gold labels, and reports the metrics to the trainer and to a CSV file::
+
+    from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer, SentenceTransformerTrainer
+    from sentence_transformers.evaluation import ClusteringEvaluator
+
+    model = SentenceTransformer("all-MiniLM-L6-v2")
+    evaluator = ClusteringEvaluator(
+        sentences=dev_sentences,
+        labels=dev_labels,
+        algorithm="kmeans",  # or "agglomerative"
+        name="topic_dev",
+    )
+    # score = evaluator(model)  # standalone evaluation
+    # Or plug it into the trainer like any other evaluator:
+    # trainer = SentenceTransformerTrainer(model=model, eval_dataset=..., evaluator=evaluator, ...)
+
+The evaluator returns a dictionary with ``v_measure``, ``adjusted_rand``, ``normalized_mutual_info``
+and ``accuracy`` (prefixed with the evaluator name), and exposes ``v_measure`` as its
+``primary_metric`` for model selection.
